Clear the ID record of the last parser node when a parse ends, so a
parse(keep.source = TRUE) that follows a lexer error cannot loop
forever in finalizeData().

R_FinalizeSrcRefState() (src/main/gram.y) zeroes the ID_ID/ID_PARENT
records that a parse wrote so the arrays can be reused by the next
parse, but it loops over 0..identifier-1 while 'identifier' is the id
of the last node, not a count.  When a lexer error (unrecognized
escape, invalid \U value, invalid multibyte character, ...) unwinds out
of a parse mid-expression, that last node is a recorded one, so its
ID_ID entry survives into the next parse that reuses the arrays.  If
the same id then lands on an unrecorded node of that parse
(expr_or_assign_or_help, prog, ...), finalizeData() takes it for a node
present in the parse data: the FD_FAST_ASSIGN_COMMENTS walk that
attaches a comment to its enclosing node reaches the node, jumps to the
stale row, whose own parent chain leads back to the node, and spins
forever:

    ALARM: working on the last Unit for 32 seconds
    #6 CHK src/main/memory.c:160
    #7 INTEGER src/main/memory.c:4209
    #8 finalizeData src/main/gram.c:6608
    #9 R_Parse src/main/gram.c:4277
    #10 R_ParseVector src/main/gram.c:4332
    #11 do_parse src/main/source.c:286

The testcase alone is instant: it only hangs after an input that died
in the lexer, which is why it needed the fuzzer's process state.  Two
parses in one top-level expression reproduce it in a plain R console:

    local({
        try(parse(text = "#a\n#a\n#a\ny + '\\q'", keep.source = TRUE),
            silent = TRUE)
        parse(text = "c#\nc#\n", keep.source = TRUE)
    })

Scripts run with Rscript or R -f are unaffected (R_ReplFile keeps the
parser state busy, so every parse() gets fresh arrays); the console
REPL and C callers like the fuzz harness reuse them.

Loop over 0..identifier inclusive.  Dates from the parse-data code in
R 3.0.0.

The upstream PR also adds a regression test (tests/reg-tests-1e.R),
not carried here.

Found by OSS-Fuzz: testcase 5381026965422080 (libFuzzer_r_parse,
timeout in finalizeData).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #378, filed 2026-10-14.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/main/gram.c
+++ b/src/main/gram.c
@@ -3955,9 +3955,15 @@
     if (PS_IDS != R_NilValue) {
 	if (ParseState.prevState || ID_COUNT > MAX_DATA_COUNT) {
 	    PS_SET_IDS(R_NilValue);
-        } else {/* Remove the parent records */
+        } else {
+	    /* Remove the parent records.  'identifier' is the id of the
+	       last node, not a count, so include it: when a lexer error
+	       unwinds mid-expression that node is a recorded one, and a
+	       stale ID_ID would make finalizeData() treat an unrecorded
+	       node of the next parse as present in its parse data, in
+	       which case the comment-parent walk can loop forever. */
             if (identifier > ID_COUNT) identifier = ID_COUNT;
-            for (int i=0; i < identifier; i++) {
+            for (int i=0; i <= identifier; i++) {
 		ID_ID(i) = 0;
 	        ID_PARENT(i) = 0;
 	    }
--- a/src/main/gram.y
+++ b/src/main/gram.y
@@ -1646,9 +1646,15 @@
     if (PS_IDS != R_NilValue) {
 	if (ParseState.prevState || ID_COUNT > MAX_DATA_COUNT) {
 	    PS_SET_IDS(R_NilValue);
-        } else {/* Remove the parent records */
+        } else {
+	    /* Remove the parent records.  'identifier' is the id of the
+	       last node, not a count, so include it: when a lexer error
+	       unwinds mid-expression that node is a recorded one, and a
+	       stale ID_ID would make finalizeData() treat an unrecorded
+	       node of the next parse as present in its parse data, in
+	       which case the comment-parent walk can loop forever. */
             if (identifier > ID_COUNT) identifier = ID_COUNT;
-            for (int i=0; i < identifier; i++) {
+            for (int i=0; i <= identifier; i++) {
 		ID_ID(i) = 0;
 	        ID_PARENT(i) = 0;
 	    }
